{parent=Paper 16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

For the example, take a once-punctured <torus> $P$, an essential nonseparating simple closed curve $c\subset P$, and
$$
M=P\times I,\qquad A=c\times I.
$$
The inclusion of the <annulus> $A$ induces the <injection> $\langle c\rangle\hookrightarrow\pi_1(P)$, so $A$ is an <incompressible surface>. Cutting gives $(P\text{ cut along }c)\times I$, a <pair of pants> times an interval. A <compact> <connected> <surface> with <boundary> times an interval is a <handlebody> of <genus> $1-\chi(P)$: cut the <surface> to a <disk> by proper arcs and reverse those cuts as one-handle attachments. Both the punctured <torus> and the pair of pants have <Euler characteristic> $-1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{M\text{ and }M\text{ cut along }A\text{ are both genus-two handlebodies}.}
$$
This <self-reproducing incompressible annulus cut> also shows why arbitrary incompressible cutting choices need not terminate.

A <three-manifold hierarchy> is a finite sequence of cuts along properly embedded two-sided <incompressible surfaces>, with no spherical components, whose final manifold is a disjoint union of <three-balls>. <Disks> are permitted. In an <orientable> ambient manifold two-sided <surfaces> are <orientable>; for an ambient manifold which is not <orientable> we permit two-sided <surfaces> without assuming their <orientability> as well.

For $S^1\times F$, first cut along $\{p\}\times F$, obtaining $I\times F$. This <surface> is two-sided and its <fundamental group> injects as a direct factor. Next choose essential two-sided curves in $F$ until the cut <surface> has <boundary>, and cut along their products with $I$. For <orientable> $F$ one nonseparating curve suffices. For $F$ which is not <orientable> and has <genus> at least two, the <boundary> of an embedded <Möbius band> is a two-sided essential curve; neither side is a <disk>, so its <fundamental group> injects. Cutting this curve leaves pieces with <boundary>, including the <Möbius band>. For each resulting <compact> <surface> choose a finite system of proper arcs cutting it into <disks>. The corresponding vertical <disks> give the remaining <three-manifold hierarchy> cuts. Essential <annuli> have <injective> <fundamental group>, and <disks> have trivial <fundamental group>, so all cuts are incompressible. The terminal products are $D^2\times I$, hence <three-balls>. This construction covers the possibilities without <orientability> in the stated range as well as the <orientable> ones.

Here is a terminating construction for a <compact> <connected> <orientable> <irreducible three-manifold> $M$ with nonempty <boundary>. We use the permitted general results in the following precise forms: a boundary-incompressible non-ball component has a <connected> two-sided nonseparating <incompressible surface> with nonempty <boundary>; and a sufficiently large collection of disjoint closed <incompressible surfaces> has two parallel components with no intervening member. These are respectively the existence and finiteness results for <incompressible surfaces>.

First perform essential <boundary> <disk> compressions as long as possible. Their number is finite: each nonseparating compression decreases <boundary> <genus>, while a separating compression divides a <boundary> component into two of smaller <genus>. More explicitly, the sum of $\max(0,2g-1)$ over the closed boundary components decreases at every essential compression. Each cut preserves <three-manifold irreducibility>. To see this for any incompressible cutter, a <sphere> in the cut manifold bounds a <three-ball> in the uncut manifold. A closed nonspherical incompressible component of the cutter cannot lie in this <three-ball>, since its <fundamental group> could not inject into the trivial group. A component with <boundary> cannot lie in an interior <three-ball> either. Thus the <three-ball> misses the cutter and survives in the cut manifold. Fundamental-group <injectivity> of two-sided <incompressible surfaces> follows from the <Loop theorem>, applied to their two <boundary> copies after cutting.

Push the <boundary> of each non-ball residual component slightly into the original $M$ to make a closed frontier $F_1$, omitting spherical components. This frontier is incompressible on its residual side, since all <boundary> compressions there have been exhausted. It is also incompressible on the side already cut off. Indeed, a hypothetical compression <disk> on that side can be made transverse to the earlier cutting <surfaces>. An innermost intersection circle bounds a <disk> on its cutting <surface> by incompressibility; replacing the corresponding subdisk removes the circle, without introducing intersections with earlier cutters. Repeat in the order of the cuts. The <disk> would finally lie in a product collar of the frontier, where its <boundary> is fundamental-group-injective, a contradiction. Thus $F_1$ is incompressible in $M$.

If a residual component is not a <three-ball>, choose the permitted nonseparating <incompressible surface> with <boundary>, cut it, and compress the new <boundary> fully again. Push that <boundary> in to obtain a new closed frontier $F_2$ disjoint from $F_1$. Continue. <Three-manifold irreducibility> persists, and the same innermost-circle proof makes every nonspherical frontier incompressible in the original $M$.

We explain why the parallel frontiers forced by the finiteness theorem cannot occur in this construction. The required product fact is: **a <connected> nonseparating <incompressible surface> in $G\times I$ cannot be proper and disjoint from $G\times\{1\}$**. To prove it, intersect the <surface> with vertical <annuli> $c\times I$ for essential curves $c$ in a closed $G$. Inessential intersection circles can be removed using incompressibility and the <three-balls> supplied by <three-manifold irreducibility>. An outermost intersection arc has both endpoints on the lower <boundary>; <boundary> compression along its annular bigon reduces the intersections. At least one resulting component stays nonseparating, since the mod-two relative class of the original <surface> is the sum of the resulting classes. Essential intersection circles can likewise be removed by surgery along the <annulus> running to its lower <boundary>. Retain a nonseparating incompressible component after each surgery. These operations reduce the intersection number and cut the base <surface> to one with <boundary>. Now intersect with the vertical <disks> of a proper arc system cutting that base to a <disk>. The same innermost-circle and outermost-arc operations make the <surface> disjoint from these <disks>. It then lies in a <three-ball> $D^2\times I$, where any proper two-sided <surface> separates. This is the contradiction proving the product fact.

If two frontiers in the construction were parallel with no other frontier between, their product region would contain one of the intervening nonseparating cutters, disjoint from the later frontier. The product fact forbids this. Consequently the closed-frontier construction has only finitely many stages. Once a residual irreducible component has only spherical <boundary>, push one <boundary> <sphere> inward. The <three-ball> it bounds cannot be on the collar side, which contains a <boundary> component of the manifold. The <three-ball> and the collar therefore exhaust that component, making it a <three-ball>. We have constructed **a finite <three-manifold hierarchy>, and can choose all its cutting <surfaces> to have nonempty <boundary>**.

We will use one further consequence in the map argument: these manifolds are <aspherical>. Work backwards through the <three-manifold hierarchy>. A <three-ball> is <contractible>. At one gluing stage, the <van Kampen theorem> normal form identifies the lifts of the cut pieces and cutting <surface> in the <universal cover> with a tree of spaces. The lifted pieces are <contractible> by induction, and the lifted cutting <surfaces> are <contractible> because the <surfaces> have <boundary> and are fundamental-group-injective. A finite subtree is <contractible> by removing terminal vertices; every <sphere> map meets only finitely many pieces. All higher <homotopy groups> therefore vanish at this stage, and induction proves <asphericity from a three-manifold hierarchy>. Apply this tree argument separately at each gluing stage.

The following three entries complete a single proof of the relative-boundary <homeomorphism> assertion.

= i
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

First show that the <boundary> map is onto and has degree $\pm1$, and that the fundamental-group <injection> is actually an <isomorphism>. An <injective> map between closed <surfaces> is a <homeomorphism> onto an open-and-closed subset by <invariance of domain>. Thus each component of $\partial M$ maps homeomorphically to a distinct component of $\partial N$.

Orient $M$ and $N$, and write
$$
f_*[M,\partial M]=d[N,\partial N].
$$
Apply the <boundary> map in the relative <homology> sequence. The <boundary> of the right side has coefficient $d$ on every <boundary> component of $N$. On the left, an image <boundary> component has coefficient $+1$ or $-1$, and an omitted component has coefficient zero. Since at least one component is present, $d=\pm1$; then no component can be omitted and all signs agree. Therefore
$$
\boxed{f|_{\partial M}:\partial M\longrightarrow\partial N\text{ is a homeomorphism},\qquad\deg f=\pm1.}
$$

Let $H=f_*\pi_1(M)$ and lift $f$ to the <connected> cover $p:N_H\to N$ corresponding to $H$. If the cover were noncompact, then $H_3(N_H,\partial N_H;\mathbb Z)=0$. The lifted relative fundamental class would have zero <boundary>, but its <boundary> is a nonzero sum of distinct <boundary> fundamental classes, since the original <boundary> map is <injective> and the <boundary> components are <compact>. This is impossible. The cover therefore has a finite number $r$ of sheets. Degree multiplicativity gives
$$
\pm1=\deg f=r\,\deg\widetilde f.
$$
Hence $r=1$. The image of $f_*$ is the whole target <fundamental group>, and the assumed <injection> makes **$f_*$ an <isomorphism>**.

= ii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose a <connected> proper <incompressible surface> $S$ with nonempty <boundary> as the first cut in a <three-manifold hierarchy> of $N$. Homotope $f$ relative to $\partial M$ into transverse position and let $F=f^{-1}(S)$.

We first make $F$ incompressible. If $D$ is a compression <disk> for $F$, its <boundary> maps to a loop in $S$ which is <null-homotopic> in $N$. Since $S$ is fundamental-group-injective, this loop is <null-homotopic> in $S$. Fill it in $S$ and alter $f$ in a small neighbourhood of $D$ so that its inverse image is obtained by compressing $F$ along $D$. The alteration is a <homotopy>: the filling and the old <disk> give a <sphere> map in $N$, which is <null-homotopic> by the asphericity proved above. A product collar of $S$ specifies the two sides of the modification. The integer $\sum_j\max(0,1-\chi(F_j))$ decreases at every essential compression; sphere components can be removed separately. A resulting <sphere> component of $F$ bounds a <three-ball> in $M$ by <three-manifold irreducibility>; move the map of that <three-ball> to one side of $S$ to remove it. The required extension and <homotopy> exist because the higher <homotopy groups> of $N$ vanish. Continuing leaves an incompressible $F$ with no spherical components.

For each component $F_j$, the map on <fundamental groups> to $S$ is <injective>. Indeed, its composition with the <injection> $\pi_1(S)\to\pi_1(N)$ equals the composition of $\pi_1(F_j)\to\pi_1(M)$ with the <isomorphism> $f_*$. Both maps in the latter composition are <injective>. A closed nonspherical <orientable> $F_j$ cannot occur: its <fundamental group> would inject into $\pi_1(S)$, a <free group>, whereas every subgroup of a <free group> is free and a closed positive-genus <surface> group is not free. Thus every component of $F$ has <boundary>.

Apply the permitted surface-map <homotopy> result to $F_j\to S$, fixing its <boundary>. A fundamental-group-injective map of <compact> <surfaces> is <homotopic> either to a covering or, in the <annulus> case here, to a map into one target <boundary> circle. The exceptional <annulus> case is impossible: its two distinct <boundary> circles would map onto the same circle of $\partial S$, contradicting the <injectivity> of the original <boundary> map. If $S$ is a <disk>, <injectivity> makes each $F_j$ a <disk>, and the unique <boundary> circle directly gives a single <disk> <homeomorphism>. Otherwise each component is <homotopic> to a cover of $S$.

Every point of $\partial S$ has exactly one preimage on $\partial F$, since $f|_{\partial M}$ is a <homeomorphism>. Every cover component contributes its positive degree to the number of preimages on every target <boundary> circle. Thus there is exactly one component and its covering degree is one. We have proved that **$F\to S$ can be homotoped to a <homeomorphism>, relative to its <boundary>**.

Extend this <surface> <homotopy> to $M$, and use the two-sided collars to make $f$ a product <homeomorphism> on a neighbourhood of $F$. The transverse normal direction is a degree-one local crossing, so the collars on its two sides match the two sides of $S$; any superfluous folds can be removed in the normal interval. We may now cut $M$ along $F$ and $N$ along $S$, obtaining a map whose restriction to the entire cut <boundary> is a <homeomorphism>. The other hypotheses also survive: the cut manifolds are irreducible, have nonempty <boundary>, and the inclusion of every cut component into its uncut manifold is fundamental-group-injective by the normal form of the <van Kampen theorem>. A loop killed by the cut map is consequently killed by $f_*$ and hence is trivial in its source cut component.

= iii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Apply the boundary-degree argument to each source cut component. It maps to one target cut component and is a <homeomorphism> on each of its <boundary> components onto distinct target <boundary> components. Its relative degree must be $\pm1$, so it meets the whole <boundary> of that target component. Since the global cut <boundary> map is one-to-one, two source components cannot map to the same target component; since it is onto, every target component is accounted for. Thus the cut components correspond bijectively, and each paired map satisfies the original hypotheses.

Induct on the remaining number of cuts in the chosen target <three-manifold hierarchy>, keeping fixed all <boundary> pieces already straightened. At the final stage the target components are <three-balls>. Each corresponding source component is irreducible with a single spherical <boundary>, and is therefore a <three-ball> by the inward-pushed-boundary argument above. Extend its prescribed <boundary> <homeomorphism> over the <three-ball> by coning. The original <three-ball> map and this <homeomorphism> are <homotopic> relative to the <boundary>: after identifying the target with a convex <three-ball>, take their straight-line <homotopy>. This establishes the induction base.

Reverse the cuts. On both copies of every cutting <surface> the two constructed maps agree with the fixed product <homeomorphism> of its collar, so the component <homeomorphisms> glue. Their <homotopies> also glue, because all cut boundaries have stayed fixed. Repeating through the <three-manifold hierarchy> gives
$$
\boxed{f\simeq h\ \text{relative to }\partial M,\qquad h:M\longrightarrow N\text{ a homeomorphism}.}
$$
The proof derives the three-dimensional rigidity from the <three-manifold hierarchy> and the permitted surface-map straightening; it does not use the desired three-dimensional <homeomorphism> assertion as a quoted theorem.
